+# CSV import
+
+## Why
+
+Sales keeps leads in spreadsheets and wants to load a whole CSV file into
+contactbook instead of adding contacts one by one. The import must say exactly
+what it did: which contacts were created, which were updated, and which rows
+were skipped or only partly applied, and why, so that the user can fix the
+spreadsheet and re-import.
+
+The file has the same columns as our CSV export (`name,email,phone,tags`, tags
+joined with `;`). It may also come from our address-book export, which has
+`first_name` + `last_name` columns instead of `name`.
+
+## Interface (fixed)
+
+- `src/contactbook/importer.py`: `import_contacts(repo: InMemoryContactRepository, data: str | bytes) -> ImportReport`
+  where `data` is the file content: raw bytes as read from disk, or already-decoded text.
+- `ImportReport` (dataclass, same module): `created: list[str]`, `updated: list[str]` (normalised
+  emails), `errors: list[RowIssue]`, `warnings: list[RowIssue]`.
+- `RowIssue` (dataclass, same module): `row: int`, `column: str | None`, `message: str`.
+- `ImportFileError(ContactError)` in `src/contactbook/errors.py`, raised when the file as a whole
+  can't be imported.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+### Whole-file outcomes
+
+1. When `ImportFileError` is raised, the repository is unchanged.
+2. An empty file (no content at all, or only whitespace and/or a BOM) raises `ImportFileError`.
+3. A file with a header row and no contact rows (or only blank rows) returns an
+   empty report: no created, updated, errors or warnings, and the repository is unchanged.
+4. A header that lacks an `email` column raises `ImportFileError`.
+5. A header that lacks a name column raises `ImportFileError`. A name column is
+   either `name`, or both `first_name` and `last_name`.
+6. A header in which a recognised column (`name`, `email`, `phone`, `tags`,
+   `first_name`, `last_name`) appears more than once raises `ImportFileError`.
+7. A file with more than 1000 contact rows raises `ImportFileError`. Blank rows
+   do not count, but rows with errors do. Exactly 1000 rows is accepted.
+8. Bytes that are not valid UTF-8 raise `ImportFileError`, and the message tells
+   the user to re-save the file as "CSV UTF-8". There is no fallback to another encoding.
+9. A file that the CSV parser cannot read raises `ImportFileError`. This
+   includes malformed quoting such as an unclosed quote, which would otherwise
+   silently swallow the rest of the file into one cell.
+
+### Encoding and format
+
+10. Bytes input is decoded as UTF-8, with or without a leading BOM.
+11. Text input that still starts with the BOM character (U+FEFF) is handled
+    the same as text without it, so the first header name is still recognised.
+12. The delimiter is a comma. Standard CSV quoting is supported, including
+    commas, quotes and line breaks inside quoted cells.
+13. LF, CRLF and CR line endings are all accepted.
+
+### Header
+
+14. Columns may appear in any order.
+15. Header names are matched case-insensitively and ignoring surrounding
+    whitespace, so `" Email "` matches `email`.
+16. `phone` and `tags` columns are optional.
+17. Unknown columns are ignored and are never an error. A non-empty unknown
+    header name produces one warning with `row` 1 and `column` None. Empty
+    header names, such as those from trailing commas, are ignored silently.
+18. If `name` is present, it is the name column. Any `first_name`/`last_name`
+    columns are then treated as unknown columns.
+
+### Row numbering and blank rows
+
+19. `RowIssue.row` is the spreadsheet row number: the header is row 1 and the
+    first contact row is row 2. Every line of the file counts, including blank
+    lines. For a record that spans several lines (a quoted multi-line cell),
+    the row is the line where the record starts.
+20. Rows where every cell is empty or only whitespace (including empty lines
+    and Excel's `,,,` lines) are skipped silently. They produce no issue and
+    are not counted toward the 1000-row limit.
+21. A row with fewer cells than the header treats the missing cells as blank.
+    Cells beyond the header's width are ignored. Neither case is an issue in itself.
+
+### Per-row validation
+
+22. `RowIssue.column` for a field problem is the lower-case field name: `name`,
+    `email`, `phone` or `tags`. A name problem in a `first_name`/`last_name`
+    file uses `name`.
+23. Email is trimmed and lower-cased with the existing email helper. A missing
+    or invalid email is an error with column `email`.
+24. Name is required on every row, including rows that update an existing
+    contact. It is validated with the existing name helper, which trims the
+    value and collapses whitespace runs. An empty or invalid name is an error
+    with column `name`.
+25. With `first_name`/`last_name`, the name is the two cells joined with one
+    space. The result is then validated as in criterion 24, so one part may be
+    blank, but not both.
+26. The tags cell is split on `;`. Each piece is normalised with the existing
+    tag helper. Empty pieces and repeats are ignored silently. Any invalid tag
+    is an error with column `tags`.
+27. A row with one or more errors is not applied at all: no create and no
+    partial update. Each invalid field (name, email, tags) gives its own error.
+28. An invalid phone is not an error. The rest of the row is applied, the
+    phone is left as it was (none for a new contact), and a warning with column
+    `phone` is added. This warning appears only if the row is applied.
+
+### Create and update
+
+29. A row whose email is not in the repository creates a contact with that
+    name, email, phone (if valid and non-blank) and tags. The normalised email
+    is appended to `created`.
+30. A row whose email is already in the repository (case-insensitive) updates
+    that contact:
+    - the name is replaced by the file's name;
+    - the phone is replaced only when the cell has a valid value. A blank cell
+      or a missing `phone` column leaves the phone unchanged;
+    - the file's tags are added to the existing tags, and an import never
+      removes a tag. A blank cell or a missing `tags` column adds nothing.
+31. If an update changes something, the normalised email is appended to
+    `updated`. If the row would leave the contact exactly as it was, the email
+    appears in neither `created` nor `updated`.
+32. `created` and `updated` list emails in file row order. Errors and warnings
+    are listed in row order.
+33. Duplicate emails in one file: the first row with a given normalised email
+    claims it. Every later row with the same email is an error with column
+    `email` that names the first row, and is not applied. This holds even if
+    the first row itself was rejected for another error.
+34. Valid rows are imported even when other rows have errors (partial import).
+35. All stored values go through the existing validation helpers, so lookups
+    by email keep working after import.
+36. Re-importing the output of `export_csv` into the repository it came from
+    changes nothing, and the report is empty.
+
+## Decisions taken on points the requester left open
+
+- Criterion 18 (`name` wins over `first_name`/`last_name` when both are present).
+- Criterion 21 (short rows are padded with blanks, extra cells are ignored).
+- Criterion 27 (one error per invalid field) and criterion 28 (no phone warning on rejected rows).
+- Criterion 33 (a rejected first row still claims its email).
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- Deleting contacts that are not in the file.
+- Clearing a phone or removing tags through import.
+- Dry run / preview mode.
+- Streaming or incremental processing.
+- Encodings other than UTF-8, and delimiters other than comma (e.g. `;` from
+  some Excel locales).
+- Formats other than CSV.
+- A CLI command or HTTP endpoint (other packages own those).
+- An import method on `ContactBook`.
